UPDATE ki_curriculum SET drill_scenario='You''re on a working session with the growth PM at a mid-market retail brand that''s launching a refer-a-friend program in the next sprint. They already use Branch for paid attribution. Their engineer is on the call and asks: ''Walk us through exactly how the referral code gets from the sharer''s link to the new user''s app so we can auto-apply the reward — we don''t want the user typing a code.''', drill_spoken_task='Explain how a Branch-powered referral link carries the referral code and sharer identity through install, and where the app reads it to apply the reward automatically.', drill_response_shape='talk_track', drill_model_answer='A Branch-powered referral program works like this: the referring user taps Share in the app, Branch generates a unique link with the referral code and user ID embedded in the link data. The new user clicks it, installs, opens the app, and Branch delivers the referral code via getFirstReferringParams in the init callback — the app applies the reward automatically. The attribution chain — who referred whom, whether the install happened, whether the referred user converted — is all tracked through Branch. For retail and fintech customers running refer-a-friend programs, this is the most common use case for Branch link personalization: every share creates a traceable, attributable link with the sharer''s identity attached.', drill_rubric='[{"c": "Names the flow: sharer taps Share → Branch link with referral_code + user_id embedded in link data", "must": true}, {"c": "Explicitly names getFirstReferringParams (or Branch init callback) as where the new app reads the code to auto-apply the reward", "must": true}, {"c": "Calls out the full attribution chain (who referred whom, install, downstream conversion) tracked through Branch", "must": true}, {"c": "Frames it as the canonical retail/fintech refer-a-friend use case for link personalization", "must": false}]'::jsonb, drill_teach_script='Here''s the tactic: making a refer-a-friend loop work through Branch links. The principle: the referral code and sharer identity ride inside the Branch link data all the way through install, so the new app can auto-apply the reward with no manual code entry. The elite line sounds like this: the sharer taps Share, Branch generates a unique link with the referral code and user ID in the link data, the new user installs and opens, and getFirstReferringParams in the init callback delivers the code to the app. So when a retail or fintech PM asks how the reward gets applied, walk the chain — link data, install, init callback, auto-apply — and name getFirstReferringParams out loud.', drill_ready=true WHERE concept_id='CH01' AND ki_id='45d46011-266e-4ac8-9c20-4b6f0c492db1';
UPDATE ki_curriculum SET drill_scenario='You''re in a QBR with a streaming media customer''s head of paid acquisition. She''s paying $6 CPI on Google App Install campaigns promoting specific shows. Post-install to first-episode-play conversion is 14% — well below her 25% target. She says, ''Users click an ad for a specific show, install, and then land on our home tab where they have to search for it again. How do we fix that?''', drill_spoken_task='Explain what deferred deep linking does for her paid app install campaigns and why it''s the fix for her drop-off.', drill_response_shape='talk_track', drill_model_answer='When a user taps an ad for a specific item — a show, a product, a flight — and they don''t have your app yet, Branch holds onto where they were trying to go through the install. The moment they open the app for the first time, we drop them on that exact screen, not your home tab. For paid acquisition that costs real money per install, that handoff is the difference between someone who converts and someone who bounces in the first ten seconds. So on your show ads, the user taps, installs, opens — and lands directly on the show''s page ready to press play. That''s deferred deep linking, and it''s the fix for the 14-to-25 gap you''re describing.', drill_rubric='[{"c": "Explains that Branch holds the intended destination through the install and delivers it on first open", "must": true}, {"c": "Ties the mechanic directly to paid-CPI economics — wasted install spend if the user bounces from the wrong landing", "must": true}, {"c": "Applies it concretely to her use case: ad for a specific show → app opens on that show''s page", "must": true}, {"c": "Names it ''deferred deep linking'' explicitly", "must": false}]'::jsonb, drill_teach_script='Here''s the tactic: deferred deep linking for paid app install campaigns. The principle: when someone taps an ad for a specific thing and doesn''t have your app, Branch holds their destination through the install and delivers them there on first open — not the home tab. The elite line sounds like this: for paid acquisition that costs real money per install, that handoff is the difference between someone who converts and someone who bounces in the first ten seconds. So when a paid-acquisition lead complains about post-install drop-off, tie the fix to CPI economics — every wasted install is real money — and name deferred deep linking as the mechanic.', drill_ready=true WHERE concept_id='C12' AND ki_id='730d4229-715a-485e-9996-f9018d6b348a';
UPDATE ki_curriculum SET drill_scenario='You''re in a technical deep-dive with a travel brand''s mobile engineering lead. Their in-house link solution keeps breaking: Instagram in-app browser strips parameters, iOS 17 changed universal link behavior, and their SMS links opened Safari instead of the app for 20% of users last quarter. He asks, skeptical: ''What are you actually doing differently that we couldn''t build ourselves in a sprint?''', drill_spoken_task='Answer his question — what''s actually different about Branch''s deep linking versus what his team could build.', drill_response_shape='talk_track', drill_model_answer='Every channel you push links through — email, SMS, social, paid ads, your marketing site, a QR on a TV spot — has its own quirks for how it opens links on a phone. Some open in an in-app browser that blocks deep links, some strip parameters, some behave differently on iOS 17 versus Android 14. Branch has solved thousands of these edge cases so that one link you generate works correctly across every one of those surfaces, on every device, every time. The customer never sees the plumbing; they just always land where you sent them. So on your Instagram-browser and iOS 17 problems — those are two of the six-thousand-plus edge cases we already handle. Your team can absolutely build a link, but keeping it working across every channel and every OS update forever is the actual job.', drill_rubric='[{"c": "Names specific edge-case surfaces: in-app browsers, parameter stripping, iOS vs Android version differences", "must": true}, {"c": "Frames Branch''s value as the accumulated coverage of thousands of edge cases across every channel/device", "must": true}, {"c": "Answers the build-vs-buy skepticism directly — building the link is easy, keeping it working forever across OS updates is the actual job", "must": true}, {"c": "Reframes the invisibility of the plumbing as the point: users just always land where you sent them", "must": false}]'::jsonb, drill_teach_script='Here''s the tactic: defending Branch on deep linking against a build-it-ourselves engineer. The principle: the real value isn''t the link — it''s the six-thousand-plus edge cases across channels, devices, and OS versions that Branch has already solved and keeps solving as the platforms change. The elite line sounds like this: Branch has solved thousands of these edge cases so one link works correctly across every surface, on every device, every time — the customer never sees the plumbing. So when an engineer says ''we could build this in a sprint,'' name specific edge cases they''re already hitting — in-app browsers, iOS 17, parameter stripping — and reframe the job as keeping it working forever, not building it once.', drill_ready=true WHERE concept_id='C13' AND ki_id='84222aef-da7a-4f02-9db9-e80bee685aac';
UPDATE ki_curriculum SET drill_scenario='You''re in a working session with a national retailer''s brand marketing director. She''s launching a holiday campaign with three thousand unique QR codes across TV spots, direct mail pieces, and in-store endcaps — each tied to a specific product category and store region. She says, ''I need every code branded to look like ours, and I need to know per-code which billboard, which mailer, which spot actually drove installs and revenue. And I don''t want my team hand-generating three thousand codes in a dashboard.''', drill_spoken_task='Explain how Branch supports her holiday QR launch — the generation, the branding, and the attribution.', drill_response_shape='talk_track', drill_model_answer='Your marketing team needs QR codes for direct mail, TV spots, in-store signage, events — thousands of them, each tied to a specific campaign, audience, or product. Branch lets you generate every one of those programmatically through our API, with full design control over how the QR looks and full attribution on every scan. The customer ends up with a branded code on their physical material that opens the right screen in the app — and you can see exactly which billboard, which mailer, which spot drove which install and which revenue event downstream. So for your three-thousand-code holiday launch, your team hits the Branch API in a script, gets back three thousand branded QRs tied to unique deep links, and you get the per-code install and revenue attribution you''re asking for.', drill_rubric='[{"c": "Names programmatic API generation as the answer to ''don''t want my team hand-generating'' at scale", "must": true}, {"c": "Covers full design/branding control on the QR itself", "must": true}, {"c": "Covers per-code attribution all the way to downstream install and revenue event, not just scans", "must": true}, {"c": "Ties the answer directly to her three-thousand-code holiday launch use case", "must": false}]'::jsonb, drill_teach_script='Here''s the tactic: selling Branch QR at scale to a brand marketing lead. The principle: programmatic generation plus branded design plus per-code attribution — the three legs that make physical-to-digital campaigns measurable instead of a blind spend. The elite line sounds like this: your team hits the Branch API, gets back thousands of branded QRs tied to unique deep links, and you see exactly which billboard, which mailer, which spot drove which install and revenue event downstream. So when a marketer asks about QR at scale, hit all three legs — API, brand control, per-code revenue attribution — and tie it back to her specific campaign scale.', drill_ready=true WHERE concept_id='C14' AND ki_id='92b7637a-9cad-4f5b-a229-4aaa426ada96';
UPDATE ki_curriculum SET drill_scenario='You''re with a travel booking platform''s head of digital. Sixty percent of their booking research happens on desktop but ninety percent of repeat bookings happen in the app. She says: ''My desktop users start a hotel search, get engaged, and then I lose them because the download flow means opening the App Store on their phone and searching for us. By the time they get there they''ve dropped. What have you got?''', drill_spoken_task='Pitch Text-Me-The-App as the fix for her desktop-to-app handoff.', drill_response_shape='talk_track', drill_model_answer='Text-Me-The-App is a feature where a web visitor enters their phone number on your mobile site and receives a text with a Branch link. They tap the link on their phone and are deep linked directly into the right screen inside the app — or, if they do not have the app, they go to the store and land on that screen after install. It closes the ''I want to download later'' gap: the user gets the link exactly when they are interested and on the right device. Configuration is in the Branch dashboard. So for your desktop hotel-search flow: the user hits an in-line prompt, enters their phone number, gets the text, opens the link on their phone, and lands directly on the exact hotel result they were looking at — not your app''s home screen and not the App Store search page.', drill_rubric='[{"c": "Describes the mechanic clearly: web enters phone → text with Branch link → tap opens app on the right screen (or store then that screen after install)", "must": true}, {"c": "Explicitly closes the desktop-to-mobile intent-handoff gap and names the moment-of-intent capture", "must": true}, {"c": "Applies it concretely to her hotel-search desktop flow — lands on the specific hotel, not the home screen or store search", "must": true}, {"c": "Names Text-Me-The-App by name and mentions dashboard configuration", "must": false}]'::jsonb, drill_teach_script='Here''s the tactic: Text-Me-The-App for the desktop-to-app handoff. The principle: capture the user''s interest on the device where they have it — desktop — and deliver the app experience on the device where it lives — their phone — with the deep link intact so they land where they were, not on your home tab. The elite line sounds like this: the user enters their number, gets the text, taps the link, and lands directly on the exact screen they were looking at — not the App Store search page. So when a digital lead describes desktop intent leaking away before app install, pitch Text-Me-The-App as the moment-of-intent handoff and land it on their specific flow.', drill_ready=true WHERE concept_id='CH05' AND ki_id='649b9023-5530-4792-b3f6-631408b37382';
UPDATE ki_curriculum SET drill_scenario='You''re in a technical review with a fintech customer''s marketing analytics lead. Their web-to-app campaigns show ninety percent of installs as ''organic'' in Branch, but their finance team is spending six figures a month on those web ads. They use Segment server-side for web tracking. She asks: ''Are your web campaigns actually broken, or is our attribution setup broken? And how do I tell?''', drill_spoken_task='Diagnose the likely root cause and tell her exactly what to check.', drill_response_shape='talk_track', drill_model_answer='For customers running web campaigns that drive app installs, Branch closes the web-to-app attribution gap. For logged-in users flowing through Segment, Branch requires a browser fingerprint collected from the web SDK to be passed alongside the Segment server-side event — this links the web click session to the app install. For logged-out users or direct web SDK implementations, Branch handles the match through probabilistic signals. The sign of a broken web attribution setup: web campaign installs showing as organic in Branch. The first diagnostic question: is the Branch web SDK initialized on every landing page that drives app installs, and if they use Segment server-side, is the fingerprint field included in the payload. So on your ninety-percent-organic number — that''s the classic broken-setup signature, not broken campaigns. Have your engineer check both: web SDK on every landing page, and browser_fingerprint_id in the Segment server-side track payload.', drill_rubric='[{"c": "Identifies web-installs-as-organic as the classic broken-attribution signature, not broken campaigns", "must": true}, {"c": "Names the two diagnostic checks: Branch web SDK on every landing page, AND browser_fingerprint_id in the Segment server-side payload", "must": true}, {"c": "Distinguishes the logged-in path (fingerprint via Segment) from the logged-out probabilistic path", "must": true}, {"c": "Reframes ''are the campaigns broken'' as ''the plumbing is broken'' — reassuring on spend", "must": false}]'::jsonb, drill_teach_script='Here''s the tactic: diagnosing a broken web-to-app attribution setup for a Segment customer. The principle: when web campaign installs show as organic in Branch, the campaigns aren''t broken — the fingerprint isn''t being passed. Logged-in users need the Branch web SDK fingerprint carried inside the Segment server-side event; logged-out uses probabilistic match. The elite line sounds like this: have your engineer check both — web SDK on every landing page, and browser_fingerprint_id in the Segment server-side track payload. So when an analytics lead points at a suspicious organic number on a paid web campaign, name the signature, name the two checks, and reassure them the spend isn''t wasted — the plumbing is.', drill_ready=true WHERE concept_id='AT07' AND ki_id='c1e61ef7-bfd4-49d0-9ee0-0592650b24cc';
UPDATE ki_curriculum SET drill_scenario='You''re on a call with a retail customer''s growth marketing lead who''s already using Journeys for a static ''Download the App'' banner. She''s about to run a Black Friday push with five paid channels, each with its own promo code, and wants the banner to render the specific promo code the user arrived with — without her team building five separate banner templates. She says: ''Can I do this on one template? Because I do not have engineering time to build five.''', drill_spoken_task='Answer her — can she, on one Journeys template, render the specific promo code from the referring link?', drill_response_shape='talk_track', drill_model_answer='Liquid tags put if/else logic inside Journeys banner templates so the copy and content change based on who the user is and how they arrived. The most common use: show different banner text to users who arrived from a paid campaign versus organic, or render a discount amount that was embedded in the referring link. If the referring link includes a promo_code key, the banner title renders that code directly. If the key is absent, a default fallback text shows instead. This moves the banner from a static download prompt to a genuinely personalized call to action — each user sees copy relevant to their specific journey, using the same Journeys template with no additional engineering work per campaign. So yes — one template, promo_code passed on each of your five paid links, liquid tag renders the right code per user, fallback text for anyone who arrives organically. No per-campaign engineering.', drill_rubric='[{"c": "Names liquid tags as if/else logic inside Journeys templates", "must": true}, {"c": "Shows the promo_code key mechanic: link carries promo_code → liquid tag renders it in title; missing key → fallback text", "must": true}, {"c": "Answers her exact question — one template covers all five campaigns, no per-campaign engineering", "must": true}, {"c": "Frames the shift from static download prompt to personalized CTA at the campaign level", "must": false}]'::jsonb, drill_teach_script='Here''s the tactic: liquid tags for dynamic Journeys banners. The principle: if/else logic runs inside one banner template, reading keys off the referring link, so the same template serves personalized copy across every campaign without engineering per campaign. The elite line sounds like this: if the referring link includes a promo_code key, the banner renders that code; if it''s absent, a default fallback shows. One template, every campaign. So when a growth lead asks whether one template can cover five paid channels with different promo codes, answer yes, walk the promo_code mechanic, and call out the fallback so they trust the organic case works.', drill_ready=true WHERE concept_id='PU04' AND ki_id='e5efa34b-b91f-4e4a-96dc-7ee364552985';
UPDATE ki_curriculum SET drill_scenario='You''re in a strategy review with a mobile gaming customer''s UA director. His paid install volume is up 30% quarter-over-quarter, but day-7 login rate on those installs dropped from 42% to 18%, and revenue per install collapsed. His CFO is asking why the acquisition team is ''growing installs but shrinking revenue.'' He asks: ''Is this a fraud problem or a targeting problem, and if it''s fraud, what does Branch actually do about it?''', drill_spoken_task='Diagnose whether the signature looks like fraud, then explain what Fraud Protect and RealScore actually do.', drill_response_shape='talk_track', drill_model_answer='Branch Fraud Protect flags installs that do not look like real humans. RealScore evaluates every install against device signals, click-to-install timing, behavioral patterns, IP reputation, and publisher history — and scores each install''s likelihood of being genuine. Installs scoring above your configured threshold are blocked from attribution: the ad network does not receive credit and the advertiser does not pay. You configure Fraud Protect in the Branch dashboard per rule: Block, Flag, or Monitor. The customer conversation opener: if a brand''s post-install event rates (purchases, logins, subscriptions) are abnormally low relative to reported installs, that is a classic fraud signal — the installs look real to the network but the users do nothing afterward. So your day-7 login collapse from forty-two to eighteen percent alongside a thirty-percent install spike is exactly that signature — fake installs the network can''t distinguish from real ones. Turn on Fraud Protect in Monitor mode first, watch the RealScore distribution by publisher for two weeks, then flip the worst offenders to Block so the network stops getting credit and you stop paying.', drill_rubric='[{"c": "Correctly reads the signature — post-install event rate collapse + install spike = classic fraud, not targeting", "must": true}, {"c": "Explains RealScore inputs (device signals, click-to-install timing, behavioral patterns, IP reputation, publisher history) and per-install scoring", "must": true}, {"c": "Explains the enforcement outcome: installs above threshold blocked from attribution → network not credited → advertiser not billed", "must": true}, {"c": "Recommends a concrete rollout (Monitor → identify offenders → Block) rather than a generic ''turn it on''", "must": false}]'::jsonb, drill_teach_script='Here''s the tactic: diagnosing install fraud and pitching Fraud Protect. The principle: when post-install event rates crater while install volume grows, it''s fraud — installs that look real to the network but do nothing after. RealScore scores every install on device, timing, behavior, IP, and publisher signals; installs above threshold get blocked from attribution, so the network isn''t credited and the advertiser isn''t billed. The elite line sounds like this: turn on Fraud Protect in Monitor mode first, watch the RealScore distribution by publisher, then flip the worst offenders to Block. So when a UA lead describes install growth but revenue collapse, name the signature out loud, walk the RealScore inputs, and give them the Monitor-then-Block rollout — don''t just say ''we have fraud detection.''', drill_ready=true WHERE concept_id='PS04' AND ki_id='431adc5a-f022-4a85-9511-8d7acc8f611c';